    "body": "Arkansas Highway Police and federal law enforcement agencies conducted a sweeping 48-hour commercial vehicle enforcement operation that resulted in 183 English-language proficiency violations, 44 criminal arrests and more than 1,000 motor carrier safety violations.\nThe crackdown, dubbed Operation Highway Shield , took place from 6 a.m. Tuesday through 6 a.m. Thursday (Oct. 27-29), targeting unsafe commercial vehicles, unqualified truck drivers and criminal activity along Arkansas highways.\nAccording to the Arkansas Department of Transportation, officers conducted 1,724 Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration inspections, placing 393 drivers and commercial motor vehicles out of service .\nAuthorities also identified more than 330 unqualified or unauthorized drivers and reported 74 immigration violations that resulted in federal immigration enforcement.\nArkansas enforcement operation by the numbers\nEnforcement category | Results\nCommercial vehicle inspections | 1,724\nDrivers and vehicles placed out of service | 393\nUnqualified or unauthorized drivers identified | More than 330\nEnglish-language proficiency disqualifications | 183\nImmigration violations resulting in federal enforcement | 74\nMotor carrier safety violations | More than 1,000\nCriminal arrests | 44\nTraffic citations and warnings | 369\nStolen trailers recovered | 1\nSource: Arkansas Department of Transportation , Oct. 29, 2026.\nDuring the two-day enforcement campaign, Arkansas Highway Police conducted enhanced inspections at designated weigh stations along interstate highways and other approved commercial vehicle corridors.\nThe operation focused on identifying unqualified drivers, unsafe vehicles, fraudulent trucking practices and criminal activity.\nOfficers also investigated suspected drug trafficking, human trafficking and smuggling, cargo theft, and stolen commercial vehicles and trailers encountered during inspections.\nAuthorities recovered one stolen trailer, although officials did not disclose its value or provide details about the circumstances surrounding the theft.\nThe state also did not provide a breakdown of the criminal arrests, the specific violations behind the out-of-service orders or the immigration enforcement outcomes.\nThe Arkansas enforcement campaign follows two previous waves of Operation Highway Shield conducted earlier this year.\nThe first operation took place in Florida in March, when inspectors conducted more than 3,300 inspections and placed 176 drivers out of service, including 54 for English-language proficiency deficiencies.\nFlorida authorities also made 35 criminal-related arrests and took 42 individuals into custody for violations of federal immigration laws.\nA second enforcement wave, conducted July 28-30 across Illinois, Indiana, Iowa and Ohio, resulted in 766 drivers and vehicles being placed out of service.\nFederal officials reported 86 arrests, 51 immigration detentions and 36 English-language proficiency violations during the Midwest operation.\nAuthorities also recovered nearly $1 million in stolen cargo.\nWhy it matters: The results highlight expanding federal-state enforcement targeting driver qualifications, trucking safety and criminal activity along major freight corridors.",
